WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:06.580
BRANDI HOUSE: Welcome
I'm Brandi House.

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:09.930
I am the product manager for
connected games at Unity.

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:12.690
And I'm joined today by three
of my friends and colleagues

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:16.329
from Google Cloud and Unity.

00:00:16.329 --> 00:00:18.400
Today, we're going to
share a little bit more

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:20.530
about what we mean
by connected games

00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:24.690
and how matchmaking fits into
that overall problem space.

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:27.450
Then Joseph will walk us
through an open match demo

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:30.600
and where the open
source project is today.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:33.750
Then Caleb will do a
demonstration of custom match

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:35.700
functions and why
we believe these

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:38.190
will open up new kinds of
innovation in matchmaking

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:39.450
today.

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:41.160
And then Micah
will finish us off

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:45.120
with an overview of the Google
Cloud ecosystem for games.

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:52.000
So what do we mean
by connected games?

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:54.640
A few of you may have seen
me and Micah talk about this

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:55.750
before.

00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:57.850
You know, it could be
single player games that

00:00:57.850 --> 00:00:59.260
are more dynamic,
because they're

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:01.180
connected through
cloud features,

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:03.670
and it could be all the
way through to MMO spaces,

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:06.710
persistent games that
are always online.

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:08.320
And the way we think
about this space,

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:11.290
the further down the chart
you go, the more cumulative

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:12.530
the problem space is.

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:15.430
So you have more technology
and more design problems

00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:17.590
that you have to solve
to be able to make

00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:19.030
those kinds of games.

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:20.270
It just doesn't get easier.

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:23.630
It only gets harder
as you go down.

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:25.590
And what we've been
hearing from game devs

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:27.630
is that the real
time multiplayer

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:30.180
space and the
persistent world spaces

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:32.730
are still too hard and
too expensive and too

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:34.860
difficult to build, especially.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:36.780
And really, the entire
space is something

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:41.360
that more and more devs
want to get connected to.

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:43.600
So today, we're focused
primarily on the real time

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:45.190
multiplayer stack.

00:01:45.190 --> 00:01:48.040
And from what we've learned,
to make them successful,

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:50.200
to get the latency,
security, scale goals

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:52.870
that your game needs,
the tech stack really

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:55.960
needs to be a dedicated
game server topology, which

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:58.570
means that you have an
entire stack from the client

00:01:58.570 --> 00:02:01.930
runtime, the networking code,
all the way up to the game

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:04.990
server hosting and management,
and all of your services

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:07.940
and how you connect all
of those pieces together.

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:10.840
So we've been collaborating
with our Google Cloud friends

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:13.990
to create new services
and new opportunities

00:02:13.990 --> 00:02:18.380
for how we glue this
entire ecosystem together.

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:22.840
And specifically, today we want
to talk more about matchmaking.

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:25.340
We've heard from many developers
that this is actually still

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:28.450
one of the hardest unsolved
problems for games.

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:30.667
At its fundamental
level, you really

00:02:30.667 --> 00:02:32.500
just need to take players
that are coming in

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:36.620
to play your game, form
quote unquote good groups,

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:38.870
and then connect them
to a game server.

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:40.760
It seems relatively simple.

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:44.480
But from what we've seen,
that definition of good

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:47.048
is actually quite subjective,
and it changes game to game.

00:02:47.048 --> 00:02:48.590
You know, if it's
a more casual game,

00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:50.900
you may care about
social graph distance.

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:52.310
If it's a hyper
competitive game,

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:54.950
you may care more and
more about skill matching.

00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:57.890
And so being able to define
what good means for your game

00:02:57.890 --> 00:03:00.050
is a surprisingly hard
problem, and there's not

00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:03.200
been a ton of innovation in the
space, because so much of it

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:06.980
is still about how do I run a
scalable technology, as opposed

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:09.662
to how do I make this
a game design problem.

00:03:09.662 --> 00:03:11.120
So from here, I'm
going to hand off

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:14.008
to Joseph, who will give
an update about Open

00:03:14.008 --> 00:03:16.550
Match and the progress of the
open source collaboration we've

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:17.217
been working on.

00:03:25.137 --> 00:03:26.220
JOSEPH: Thank you, Brandi.

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:28.090
Can we switch over
to the demo computer?

00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:28.590
Great.

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:35.730
All right, so today, I'd
like to briefly highlight

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:37.410
some of the recent
things we've been

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:40.580
doing in Open Match in
the open source version.

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:43.840
I'll give you a little bit of
a preview of upcoming features.

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:46.740
So what you're looking at
right now is some metrics.

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:48.480
I have a proof of
concept matchmaker

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:52.560
that's running in
Kubernetes on Google Cloud.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:57.190
I've seeded it with about
60,000 random players or so.

00:03:57.190 --> 00:03:58.800
So this is pretty
similar to the demo

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:02.202
that we gave at Unite
Berlin last year.

00:04:02.202 --> 00:04:04.160
If any of you have seen
that, it is on YouTube.

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:06.102
I highly recommend you watch it.

00:04:06.102 --> 00:04:07.560
This one is a little
bit different.

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:10.270
It's sending four
requests at a time,

00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:12.660
instead of one
request at a time you

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:15.560
can see it's churning through
about 10 loops per minute,

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:17.670
four requests per
loop, 10 or 11 loops.

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:22.650
So it's getting about
45 matchmaking loops

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:24.010
going per minute.

00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:26.250
That's the bottom left hand.

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:30.420
The top right hand is
approximately how many players

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:32.970
are getting matched per minute.

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:34.600
Sorry, top left.

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:39.090
Top right is roughly how long
each request that's going up

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:40.350
against open match is taking.

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:44.190
You can see here they're taking
a little less than two seconds.

00:04:44.190 --> 00:04:46.770
In the bottom right,
sort of shows you

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:49.533
how many of the requests are
actually finding a match.

00:04:49.533 --> 00:04:51.700
You can see there's a red
line at the bottom showing

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:55.410
that nothing is not
finding a match right now.

00:04:55.410 --> 00:05:01.680
So four requests at a
time is not particularly

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:03.570
high by Open Match standards.

00:05:03.570 --> 00:05:05.670
What we really want
is to be able to get

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:09.000
a lot of concurrent processing
done simultaneously.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:14.040
So let me bump this up
from four to about 400,

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:16.800
and we're going to see
what happens to the graphs.

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:19.470
Hopefully everything
should scale out smoothly.

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:22.560
The idea here is we're
going to slice up

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:25.050
the parts of the
player pool that we're

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:27.360
looking at into smaller
and smaller pieces

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:29.910
and look for a match
within each one of those

00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:32.580
all at the same time
concurrently across lots

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:35.310
of processes in a
Kubernetes cluster.

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:41.220
So some recent work that
Unity has finished and then

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:44.160
contributed the design back
to the open source project

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:50.010
for making matchmaking functions
stay alive in the Kubernetes

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:52.620
cluster and not spin
down in between cycles,

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:55.470
so that you get much
lower latency matching

00:05:55.470 --> 00:05:58.390
is really helping us bring
this to life right now.

00:05:58.390 --> 00:06:01.410
So unfortunately,
my Grafana instance

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:03.083
is set to only refresh
every 30 seconds.

00:06:03.083 --> 00:06:05.250
So we're going to have to
be slightly patient to see

00:06:05.250 --> 00:06:06.900
the big numbers come up.

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:08.400
But hopefully, as
you can see here,

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:12.150
like we're able to
run a few hundred--

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:15.600
sometimes this will
get up over 1,000--

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:18.690
matchmaking
functions per minute.

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:21.330
And that's able to put,
you know, tens of thousands

00:06:21.330 --> 00:06:24.720
of players through the
system per minute, finding

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:25.830
matches for them.

00:06:25.830 --> 00:06:29.130
This is kind of more
about going wide.

00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:30.840
Caleb is going to talk
a little bit later

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:34.470
about customizing the way that
you're looking for players

00:06:34.470 --> 00:06:39.280
and trying to make each search
that you're doing go faster.

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:41.820
This is more about kicking
off as many searches

00:06:41.820 --> 00:06:43.810
as you can at a time.

00:06:43.810 --> 00:06:47.010
So the last thing I'd
bring your attention to,

00:06:47.010 --> 00:06:48.960
in the bottom right,
you can see the red line

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:51.040
is starting to creep up now.

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:54.780
So what that signifies is in
certain parts of the player

00:06:54.780 --> 00:06:57.820
population, we've found
every available match there.

00:06:57.820 --> 00:07:00.520
So when we're looking in that
part of the player population,

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:02.977
for example, if you think
of like a tiered game

00:07:02.977 --> 00:07:05.310
like a competitive shooter
like "Overwatch" or something

00:07:05.310 --> 00:07:08.700
like that, this red
in the bottom corner

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:11.400
is representing like
your grandmasters

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:13.920
or your very low bronze
players, like we have found all

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:15.380
the potential matches for that.

00:07:15.380 --> 00:07:17.630
There's not enough players
in the pool to make anymore

00:07:17.630 --> 00:07:19.810
matches at that part
of the population.

00:07:19.810 --> 00:07:21.840
So that's where that's
showing in the bottom.

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:23.048
Can we go back to the slides?

00:07:25.830 --> 00:07:26.330
Great.

00:07:26.330 --> 00:07:27.860
So now I'm going to
pass it over to Caleb,

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:30.277
and he's going to talk about
customizing those matchmaking

00:07:30.277 --> 00:07:30.835
functions.

00:07:36.557 --> 00:07:37.890
CALEB ATWOOD: Thank you, Joseph.

00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:38.557
My name's Caleb.

00:07:38.557 --> 00:07:40.473
I'm a technical lead on
matchmaking at Unity.

00:07:40.473 --> 00:07:41.890
And we've been
working with Google

00:07:41.890 --> 00:07:44.240
on Open Match from
the beginning.

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:47.380
We want to solve technically
challenging problems for game

00:07:47.380 --> 00:07:49.727
developers and make
those solutions generally

00:07:49.727 --> 00:07:51.310
available to game
developers at large.

00:07:51.310 --> 00:07:53.110
In this case, as
Joseph was showing,

00:07:53.110 --> 00:07:54.610
some of these
innovations are coming

00:07:54.610 --> 00:07:58.670
in the form of services
infrastructure for matchmaking.

00:07:58.670 --> 00:08:00.480
And so what makes Open Match--

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:04.000
here's the clicker-- such
a good fit for this problem

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:07.120
is that its core has this
highly customizable component

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:09.445
called Match Functions.

00:08:09.445 --> 00:08:11.820
So we've been talking a little
bit about Match Functions,

00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:14.370
but there's some examples
on the repository,

00:08:14.370 --> 00:08:17.692
but not really a cohesive
guide for how to author one.

00:08:17.692 --> 00:08:19.150
So over the next
couple of minutes,

00:08:19.150 --> 00:08:21.780
I wanted to try and
demystify match functions

00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:22.980
a little bit for you.

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:24.690
It helps to start with this--

00:08:24.690 --> 00:08:26.850
a little bit of a
simplified services model

00:08:26.850 --> 00:08:29.835
for how to think about
matchmaking services.

00:08:29.835 --> 00:08:32.460
So on the left, you'll have your
game client with your players.

00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:34.860
They'll broadcast to the
service a type of intent.

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:36.750
This could be thought
of as like a game mode,

00:08:36.750 --> 00:08:37.958
some quality of service data.

00:08:37.958 --> 00:08:40.049
It's a regional
game service where

00:08:40.049 --> 00:08:41.789
your game servers are hosted.

00:08:41.789 --> 00:08:43.559
These will go through
a front door, which

00:08:43.559 --> 00:08:46.050
will provide the secured
data from some type

00:08:46.050 --> 00:08:47.610
of persistent database.

00:08:47.610 --> 00:08:50.393
Think like skill,
history, inventory.

00:08:50.393 --> 00:08:52.560
These are things that you
don't really want the game

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:54.795
client to be trusted to submit.

00:08:54.795 --> 00:08:57.420
At this point, the service will
package those up and send those

00:08:57.420 --> 00:08:59.292
into the Open Match front door.

00:08:59.292 --> 00:09:00.750
The Open Match
front door is really

00:09:00.750 --> 00:09:02.970
good at indexing
that data, making

00:09:02.970 --> 00:09:06.630
it highly available through a
query API for the actual Match

00:09:06.630 --> 00:09:07.170
Functions.

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:09.690
We'll get to more on the
Match Functions in a second.

00:09:09.690 --> 00:09:13.590
That's the blocks of
these blue blobs here.

00:09:13.590 --> 00:09:16.555
What's nice is Open Match takes
care of kind of activating

00:09:16.555 --> 00:09:18.930
those functions, handling the
cadence of those functions,

00:09:18.930 --> 00:09:20.520
kind of as Joseph
was showing, we

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:23.250
can achieve this high breadth
of distributed processing.

00:09:23.250 --> 00:09:25.500
And so you only have to worry
about this little atomic

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:27.200
component in that.

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:29.220
The end results of
a Match Function

00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:32.160
is that it produces a
couple like proposals,

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:33.150
you can think of them.

00:09:33.150 --> 00:09:35.175
Open Match resolves the
collision of proposals,

00:09:35.175 --> 00:09:37.410
so if players appear in
more than one proposal,

00:09:37.410 --> 00:09:39.270
it is a distributed
system after all,

00:09:39.270 --> 00:09:41.190
Open Match will
actually give you

00:09:41.190 --> 00:09:44.100
the ability to de-collide
those, and out the back end come

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:45.470
matches.

00:09:45.470 --> 00:09:47.350
And so there's two
pieces to this.

00:09:47.350 --> 00:09:49.860
One is understanding
the player data.

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:52.350
And then the other side
is the actual offering

00:09:52.350 --> 00:09:53.770
of the Match Functions.

00:09:53.770 --> 00:09:56.250
And so we'll start
with player data.

00:09:56.250 --> 00:09:58.542
Scale is kind of the
super common candidate

00:09:58.542 --> 00:09:59.500
for talking about this.

00:09:59.500 --> 00:10:02.790
It's something we all have
a rudimentary sense for.

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:05.580
And while we're not going to
talk about skills specifically,

00:10:05.580 --> 00:10:09.810
this is kind of just an example
of some distributed data

00:10:09.810 --> 00:10:11.790
that you might have in
your-- or distribution

00:10:11.790 --> 00:10:14.310
of data you would have in
your matchmaking system

00:10:14.310 --> 00:10:16.280
at any given time.

00:10:16.280 --> 00:10:18.475
And so from a match
function perspective,

00:10:18.475 --> 00:10:19.850
we're talking
about segmentation.

00:10:19.850 --> 00:10:22.518
So we're going to look at this.

00:10:22.518 --> 00:10:24.060
When you're Match
Function activates,

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:26.900
you're going to want to take
like a slice of this data,

00:10:26.900 --> 00:10:28.393
and not like the full--

00:10:28.393 --> 00:10:29.810
like the middle
one is super tall.

00:10:29.810 --> 00:10:30.860
There's a lot of
players in there.

00:10:30.860 --> 00:10:32.960
So you'd even take a
smaller slice of that,

00:10:32.960 --> 00:10:34.543
and the Match Function
is mostly going

00:10:34.543 --> 00:10:38.240
to focus on taking those
10 to 100 players or so.

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:40.280
If you put those
together, arguably that

00:10:40.280 --> 00:10:44.000
would be possibly a close match,
if it's pretty comprehensive

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:45.813
skill system.

00:10:45.813 --> 00:10:47.480
And so if we move a
little further back,

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:50.313
we can start to identify kind
of the core set of player data

00:10:50.313 --> 00:10:51.730
you're going to
need to be worried

00:10:51.730 --> 00:10:55.460
about for like a basic
matchmaking experience.

00:10:55.460 --> 00:10:57.670
And so on the top right
here, we have skill.

00:10:57.670 --> 00:10:59.620
Below that is wait
time, which is

00:10:59.620 --> 00:11:04.800
kind of an interesting one from
a Match Function perspective.

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:06.960
For this particular
metric, you might

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:09.090
want to have a Match
Function, for example,

00:11:09.090 --> 00:11:10.935
that is going to
specifically focus

00:11:10.935 --> 00:11:13.560
on the right side of this chart,
to find players that have been

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:17.280
waiting a long time for matches
and then specifically target

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:19.020
those players with
proposals that

00:11:19.020 --> 00:11:21.420
have a higher urgency or
priority to provide those

00:11:21.420 --> 00:11:23.430
back to Open Match.

00:11:23.430 --> 00:11:25.350
Open Match provides
this evaluator concept

00:11:25.350 --> 00:11:27.840
that allows you to
use that priority

00:11:27.840 --> 00:11:31.860
to prefer those matches
over other matches

00:11:31.860 --> 00:11:33.480
that players might
be appearing in.

00:11:33.480 --> 00:11:35.383
So this helps solve for--

00:11:35.383 --> 00:11:37.800
I don't have a deterministic
way to understand why players

00:11:37.800 --> 00:11:39.540
aren't getting matches quickly.

00:11:39.540 --> 00:11:41.880
Now, if you've worked in
a dedicated game server

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:43.460
architecture, on
the top left, you

00:11:43.460 --> 00:11:46.210
recognize some of these numbers.

00:11:46.210 --> 00:11:49.380
So these are going to be round
trip times to regional data

00:11:49.380 --> 00:11:51.523
centers, where your
game servers can be ran.

00:11:51.523 --> 00:11:53.190
This is super important
for matchmaking,

00:11:53.190 --> 00:11:55.650
because you want to
take these players that

00:11:55.650 --> 00:11:58.050
have an actual like
location on Earth.

00:11:58.050 --> 00:12:01.630
They have that last mile they
have to be concerned about.

00:12:01.630 --> 00:12:03.130
And so you can find
that common node

00:12:03.130 --> 00:12:04.505
that they're all
going to connect

00:12:04.505 --> 00:12:06.150
to get that tight,
low latency gameplay

00:12:06.150 --> 00:12:08.080
that you want in your game.

00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:12.330
So for a Match Function,
I could say, give me

00:12:12.330 --> 00:12:15.690
players that have less
than 120 millisecond ping

00:12:15.690 --> 00:12:18.777
to US West with
this slice of skill,

00:12:18.777 --> 00:12:19.860
with an average wait time.

00:12:19.860 --> 00:12:24.730
And if that produced eight
or 80 matches as proposals,

00:12:24.730 --> 00:12:29.088
those would possibly be
like basic ideal matches.

00:12:29.088 --> 00:12:30.880
But of course, this--
and this doesn't even

00:12:30.880 --> 00:12:31.750
scratch the surface.

00:12:31.750 --> 00:12:34.558
You have tens, hundreds,
thousands of data points

00:12:34.558 --> 00:12:36.850
that you care about when
you're doing your matchmaking,

00:12:36.850 --> 00:12:39.252
because good really does
depend on what you're

00:12:39.252 --> 00:12:40.960
trying to do with your
game, whether it's

00:12:40.960 --> 00:12:43.510
a social experience or a
super competitive experience,

00:12:43.510 --> 00:12:47.490
so level, reputation,
attach, inventory.

00:12:47.490 --> 00:12:49.760
One of my personal favorites
is current win streak.

00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:52.250
So if I go into a game,
I've won nine games.

00:12:52.250 --> 00:12:54.970
I'm going to go against a set of
players possibly who have also

00:12:54.970 --> 00:12:56.925
won nine games in a row.

00:12:56.925 --> 00:12:58.425
And so that's really
what Open Match

00:12:58.425 --> 00:13:01.550
is trying to solve
is taking away

00:13:01.550 --> 00:13:04.220
some of the technical
difficulties of running

00:13:04.220 --> 00:13:06.470
a matchmaking service
and provide that sandbox

00:13:06.470 --> 00:13:08.600
for creating a powerful,
custom way to bring

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:10.415
your players together.

00:13:10.415 --> 00:13:11.790
So now we can talk
about actually

00:13:11.790 --> 00:13:14.110
writing Match Functions.

00:13:14.110 --> 00:13:17.682
This is by no means like a
prescriptive way to do this.

00:13:17.682 --> 00:13:19.140
If you first get
started, though, I

00:13:19.140 --> 00:13:24.030
have a suspicion they'll
mostly look in this form.

00:13:24.030 --> 00:13:28.250
So step zero is to describe
your data, make your data,

00:13:28.250 --> 00:13:29.660
and design it.

00:13:29.660 --> 00:13:32.510
Anything that you can preempt as
an index going into Open Match,

00:13:32.510 --> 00:13:34.593
that's definitely going
to be preferable to trying

00:13:34.593 --> 00:13:36.830
to build that on runtime
out of segmentation

00:13:36.830 --> 00:13:38.590
that you've been creating.

00:13:38.590 --> 00:13:41.010
Then once you're in
the actual function

00:13:41.010 --> 00:13:42.440
that you've been
activated, you'll

00:13:42.440 --> 00:13:46.270
want to build and
run this query.

00:13:46.270 --> 00:13:49.320
When you get back
your tickets, you'll

00:13:49.320 --> 00:13:53.100
want to do some type
of optimization, most

00:13:53.100 --> 00:13:54.630
algorithms between
two and three,

00:13:54.630 --> 00:13:57.840
it's kind of whatever
you'd like to do.

00:13:57.840 --> 00:14:00.192
A lot of algorithms will
have you like build hash maps

00:14:00.192 --> 00:14:02.400
and do normalization on the
pool that you're actually

00:14:02.400 --> 00:14:03.010
working with.

00:14:03.010 --> 00:14:05.820
So you can kind of create
that richer algorithm.

00:14:05.820 --> 00:14:10.840
Three, put the players together.

00:14:10.840 --> 00:14:12.510
An example that
I'm going to show,

00:14:12.510 --> 00:14:14.970
I just use a
bucketization mechanism,

00:14:14.970 --> 00:14:19.687
where if the ticket fits into
a bucket, you go in the bucket.

00:14:19.687 --> 00:14:21.270
And if there's not
room in the bucket,

00:14:21.270 --> 00:14:22.900
you go in the next bucket.

00:14:22.900 --> 00:14:25.980
And so it seems
kind of rudimentary,

00:14:25.980 --> 00:14:29.400
but it actually shows a
pretty interesting result.

00:14:29.400 --> 00:14:32.210
And the last thing you can do
inside of your match function

00:14:32.210 --> 00:14:34.040
is you have an
opportunity to put

00:14:34.040 --> 00:14:35.250
a little extra data in there.

00:14:35.250 --> 00:14:37.982
So if the players asked
or have preferences

00:14:37.982 --> 00:14:39.440
for the map they're
going to be on,

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:41.482
now might be a time that
you would want to, like,

00:14:41.482 --> 00:14:44.358
roll the die and
actually assign what

00:14:44.358 --> 00:14:46.400
map they're going to play
on, so that the server,

00:14:46.400 --> 00:14:48.773
when it gets that information,
it's already configured.

00:14:51.950 --> 00:14:54.470
And so with this completely
bespoke approach,

00:14:54.470 --> 00:14:56.720
it really opens up a lot of
options that are sometimes

00:14:56.720 --> 00:14:59.420
difficult to achieve with a
configuration or rules based

00:14:59.420 --> 00:15:00.830
matchmaker.

00:15:00.830 --> 00:15:03.200
For instance, I
mentioned rolling die,

00:15:03.200 --> 00:15:05.937
combining your data
or making decisions

00:15:05.937 --> 00:15:07.520
based on the population
you're working

00:15:07.520 --> 00:15:09.560
with inside of that query.

00:15:09.560 --> 00:15:12.290
So for example, I
query for skill,

00:15:12.290 --> 00:15:14.918
but the confidence
of that skill isn't

00:15:14.918 --> 00:15:16.460
very good for the
players I got back.

00:15:16.460 --> 00:15:17.510
It's a new game.

00:15:17.510 --> 00:15:18.470
I shipped a new mode.

00:15:18.470 --> 00:15:19.970
I don't know how good they are.

00:15:19.970 --> 00:15:21.387
At that point, I
could technically

00:15:21.387 --> 00:15:23.030
switch to relying
on another metric

00:15:23.030 --> 00:15:24.800
to try and figure out whether
or not this players should

00:15:24.800 --> 00:15:25.470
play together.

00:15:25.470 --> 00:15:28.405
And I can do that inside
of the same Match Function.

00:15:28.405 --> 00:15:30.530
This is also a good
opportunity to inject something

00:15:30.530 --> 00:15:31.770
like a social graph.

00:15:31.770 --> 00:15:34.050
So if you have your
own social graph,

00:15:34.050 --> 00:15:37.020
you could make a highly
performant off box call.

00:15:37.020 --> 00:15:39.510
Has to be pretty quick.

00:15:39.510 --> 00:15:41.870
We could try and resolve
interesting connections

00:15:41.870 --> 00:15:44.370
that you can form out of the
population you're working with.

00:15:47.230 --> 00:15:47.860
There we go.

00:15:47.860 --> 00:15:50.215
All right, so if we can switch
over to the demo laptop.

00:15:53.210 --> 00:15:55.580
So showing code
on stage is always

00:15:55.580 --> 00:15:56.820
an interesting challenge.

00:15:56.820 --> 00:16:00.560
I do my best not to show
just a huge mess of spaghetti

00:16:00.560 --> 00:16:02.570
that's hard to grok.

00:16:02.570 --> 00:16:06.260
So I apologize if some of these
functions seem very helpery,

00:16:06.260 --> 00:16:08.840
but I think they kind of
highlight the lifecycle of what

00:16:08.840 --> 00:16:10.530
a function might look like.

00:16:10.530 --> 00:16:11.780
And so when you're writing
your Match Function,

00:16:11.780 --> 00:16:14.060
you'll likely be working in
something like a harness.

00:16:14.060 --> 00:16:15.967
Open Match, we're
working on providing

00:16:15.967 --> 00:16:18.050
a whole suite of harnesses
for different languages

00:16:18.050 --> 00:16:19.070
you're working with.

00:16:19.070 --> 00:16:23.870
In this case, a lot of folks
who use Unity know C# and .NET,

00:16:23.870 --> 00:16:25.940
so we have a harness
we're working with .NET.

00:16:25.940 --> 00:16:28.698
In this case, it's this
configurable Match Function.

00:16:28.698 --> 00:16:30.740
And as you can see, it
has this execute function.

00:16:30.740 --> 00:16:34.430
This is your insurance
to your Match Function.

00:16:34.430 --> 00:16:37.403
We will receive a kind of
context object from Open Match.

00:16:37.403 --> 00:16:39.320
This will contain some
interesting information

00:16:39.320 --> 00:16:41.120
about how it was
activated, some filters

00:16:41.120 --> 00:16:42.740
that the back end created.

00:16:42.740 --> 00:16:45.950
I have a query interface
for accessing my ticket data

00:16:45.950 --> 00:16:49.135
and executing those queries
on those indexes I mentioned.

00:16:49.135 --> 00:16:51.260
And then we have this custom
piece of configuration

00:16:51.260 --> 00:16:55.320
that I've submitted through a
dev dashboard in the back end.

00:16:55.320 --> 00:16:57.050
And so this will
mostly follow what

00:16:57.050 --> 00:16:59.330
I was showing you a second ago.

00:16:59.330 --> 00:17:00.920
We're going to
submit this query.

00:17:00.920 --> 00:17:03.020
As I said, the context
contains these filters

00:17:03.020 --> 00:17:04.609
that I've made on the back end.

00:17:04.609 --> 00:17:06.650
I will collect my tickets.

00:17:06.650 --> 00:17:08.300
And then these two
functions, which

00:17:08.300 --> 00:17:10.430
are those helpery functions
I was talking about,

00:17:10.430 --> 00:17:13.460
place tickets on teams is
just going to take a bucket.

00:17:13.460 --> 00:17:15.500
If you fit, you
go in the bucket.

00:17:15.500 --> 00:17:17.569
And then I'm going to
basically expand and make

00:17:17.569 --> 00:17:20.680
a whole bunch of buckets.

00:17:20.680 --> 00:17:22.450
For the second one
here, place teams

00:17:22.450 --> 00:17:25.540
on proposals, my Match Function
is actually configurable.

00:17:25.540 --> 00:17:27.230
That's what this is up here.

00:17:27.230 --> 00:17:29.150
So depending on the
number of teams.

00:17:29.150 --> 00:17:32.020
I want in this game, it will
then take as many buckets

00:17:32.020 --> 00:17:34.280
as I need and collapse
those into a proposal.

00:17:34.280 --> 00:17:37.630
So if I need 20, it'll take
20 buckets put them together.

00:17:37.630 --> 00:17:42.133
Now, opportunity
for customization.

00:17:42.133 --> 00:17:44.800
Before I put a ticket on a team,
there's an interesting gameplay

00:17:44.800 --> 00:17:46.720
design that I want introduce.

00:17:46.720 --> 00:17:49.540
My game is very creative,
collaborative game.

00:17:49.540 --> 00:17:51.790
I like it when players
have an opportunity

00:17:51.790 --> 00:17:54.255
to interact with people that
they like interacting with.

00:17:54.255 --> 00:17:55.630
But in this
particular case, it's

00:17:55.630 --> 00:17:57.110
a very collaborative
environment.

00:17:57.110 --> 00:17:59.363
So I also want them
to identify folks

00:17:59.363 --> 00:18:01.780
that they might not want to
keep playing with from session

00:18:01.780 --> 00:18:03.140
to session.

00:18:03.140 --> 00:18:07.010
And so into my
party concept, I've

00:18:07.010 --> 00:18:11.540
introduced this idea of
a list of other parties

00:18:11.540 --> 00:18:13.770
that I'd like to not
continue matchmaking with.

00:18:13.770 --> 00:18:16.400
So in other words,
I was playing,

00:18:16.400 --> 00:18:19.060
I had a great group going,
we were all talking,

00:18:19.060 --> 00:18:21.850
and it's not that someone
wasn't collaborating.

00:18:21.850 --> 00:18:23.050
They just weren't talking.

00:18:23.050 --> 00:18:27.910
So I want to play with more
people who like talking.

00:18:27.910 --> 00:18:30.400
So what I've done here
is I've introduced

00:18:30.400 --> 00:18:32.230
a hash map of those party IDs.

00:18:32.230 --> 00:18:34.570
And I simply de-collide
for these two here.

00:18:34.570 --> 00:18:36.187
Am I avoided, so
does the party, team

00:18:36.187 --> 00:18:38.770
I'm trying to match make into,
do they have players that don't

00:18:38.770 --> 00:18:43.540
want to play with me, and do
I avoid them, so these will be

00:18:43.540 --> 00:18:45.790
are their parties inside
that team that I have elected

00:18:45.790 --> 00:18:49.380
not to want to play with.

00:18:49.380 --> 00:18:52.470
And then we will take note
of some interesting metrics

00:18:52.470 --> 00:18:54.540
when we actually
run this function.

00:18:54.540 --> 00:18:56.790
And just to clarify,
this concept

00:18:56.790 --> 00:18:58.150
isn't quite like reputation.

00:18:58.150 --> 00:19:00.720
I'm not identifying people
in my community who are

00:19:00.720 --> 00:19:03.000
quote unquote bad or poisonous.

00:19:03.000 --> 00:19:05.160
This is kind of just a
self-elected mechanism

00:19:05.160 --> 00:19:07.650
that your players can use to
avoid players that they just

00:19:07.650 --> 00:19:09.710
don't want to play with.

00:19:09.710 --> 00:19:13.400
And so because this
is just a function,

00:19:13.400 --> 00:19:16.780
and I'm writing this right
next to my own game logic,

00:19:16.780 --> 00:19:18.190
I can write unit tests for this.

00:19:18.190 --> 00:19:20.290
I can mock my data layer.

00:19:20.290 --> 00:19:23.590
I can verify that when I change
something, I don't regress.

00:19:23.590 --> 00:19:26.380
And so this is a very
powerful way to test your,

00:19:26.380 --> 00:19:28.690
like, matchmaking logic.

00:19:28.690 --> 00:19:30.840
So here I've mocked
my ticket interface

00:19:30.840 --> 00:19:32.208
with some memory data.

00:19:32.208 --> 00:19:33.750
I have this function
that I'm calling

00:19:33.750 --> 00:19:34.917
that just generates traffic.

00:19:34.917 --> 00:19:39.120
In this case, I'm generating
10,000 tickets of randomly one

00:19:39.120 --> 00:19:40.650
to four players on each ticket.

00:19:40.650 --> 00:19:43.020
Now, in addition
to that, I'm also

00:19:43.020 --> 00:19:45.780
generating one to 10
random other parties

00:19:45.780 --> 00:19:47.590
that each ticket
is trying to avoid.

00:19:47.590 --> 00:19:50.580
And so there's a
whole bunch of players

00:19:50.580 --> 00:19:53.490
self-selecting into this
interesting game mechanic.

00:19:53.490 --> 00:19:55.590
Now, here's that custom
config I mentioned.

00:19:55.590 --> 00:19:58.650
In this case, I'm having 16
to 20 players on the team,

00:19:58.650 --> 00:20:01.410
and there are four
teams per match.

00:20:01.410 --> 00:20:03.640
And with this, I can
actually run this function,

00:20:03.640 --> 00:20:06.000
and we can see how quickly
it executes and how it does.

00:20:08.920 --> 00:20:10.030
Everyone can see that OK.

00:20:10.030 --> 00:20:11.440
Great.

00:20:11.440 --> 00:20:13.690
So as you can see,
there are 20,000 players

00:20:13.690 --> 00:20:16.120
that we're currently
matchmaking in this pool.

00:20:16.120 --> 00:20:19.875
Now, I have a filter here
for US West good ping.

00:20:19.875 --> 00:20:21.250
So in other words,
I'm only going

00:20:21.250 --> 00:20:23.820
to get players that are
near my US West data center.

00:20:23.820 --> 00:20:27.020
4,000 tickets about
technically qualified.

00:20:27.020 --> 00:20:28.520
It looks like only
one ticket wasn't

00:20:28.520 --> 00:20:30.850
able to fit into a given match.

00:20:30.850 --> 00:20:34.540
And I actually was able
to avoid eight match-ups

00:20:34.540 --> 00:20:36.670
that if I hadn't done
anything, those players would

00:20:36.670 --> 00:20:38.140
have been stuck playing
with players they

00:20:38.140 --> 00:20:39.505
didn't want to play with again.

00:20:39.505 --> 00:20:41.297
While this doesn't seem
like a high number,

00:20:41.297 --> 00:20:44.290
this compounds over the
course of your game cycle.

00:20:44.290 --> 00:20:46.330
If I'm able to avoid
eight every single loop

00:20:46.330 --> 00:20:49.060
or 16 every single loop,
we're talking about thousands

00:20:49.060 --> 00:20:53.440
of improved matches that day.

00:20:53.440 --> 00:20:56.610
So the end results of this
was 400 proposals, which

00:20:56.610 --> 00:21:00.580
are potential matches to Open
Match, which is quite a few.

00:21:00.580 --> 00:21:03.588
And this only took 148
milliseconds to run.

00:21:03.588 --> 00:21:05.130
Now there's some
optimizations that I

00:21:05.130 --> 00:21:06.540
could make around my bucketing.

00:21:06.540 --> 00:21:11.100
It's pretty-- not a particularly
advanced bucketing system.

00:21:11.100 --> 00:21:14.275
But as long as you keep your
performance between here

00:21:14.275 --> 00:21:15.900
and like a second or
so, you're looking

00:21:15.900 --> 00:21:18.750
at a pretty quick matchmaking
turnover, especially

00:21:18.750 --> 00:21:20.870
once you get distributed.

00:21:20.870 --> 00:21:22.560
So that's the end of the code.

00:21:22.560 --> 00:21:23.945
If I get back to
the demo slides.

00:21:26.750 --> 00:21:31.140
Awesome, and so a
Multiplay matchmaking.

00:21:31.140 --> 00:21:34.890
Built on Open Match, we've been
working on this handy service

00:21:34.890 --> 00:21:37.680
that helps bring dedicated
game server developments

00:21:37.680 --> 00:21:40.890
and scaling, but with that
super, powerful, and accessible

00:21:40.890 --> 00:21:43.110
experience you've come
to expect from Unity.

00:21:43.110 --> 00:21:45.570
We've developed this new
Multiplay matchmaking system,

00:21:45.570 --> 00:21:48.060
so it fully integrates
Multiplay's phenomenal world

00:21:48.060 --> 00:21:49.353
scaling technology.

00:21:49.353 --> 00:21:50.770
It's still Open
Match at the core.

00:21:50.770 --> 00:21:53.400
So you still get access to the
same customizable components,

00:21:53.400 --> 00:21:56.040
like custom Match Functions
and custom configuration,

00:21:56.040 --> 00:21:58.500
alongside that tooling
and lifecycle management

00:21:58.500 --> 00:22:02.410
experience that'll be coming
to the Unity ecosystem.

00:22:02.410 --> 00:22:03.732
Of course, it's a host service.

00:22:03.732 --> 00:22:05.940
That means, you don't have
to worry about operations,

00:22:05.940 --> 00:22:09.380
scaling, monitoring.

00:22:09.380 --> 00:22:13.547
And because it's a service,
it's also engine agnostic.

00:22:13.547 --> 00:22:15.130
So no matter where
you're coming from,

00:22:15.130 --> 00:22:17.050
whether it's Unity
or something else,

00:22:17.050 --> 00:22:20.230
you can get started with
Multiplay right away right.

00:22:20.230 --> 00:22:22.840
With that, I will let Micah
talk more about the cloud gaming

00:22:22.840 --> 00:22:24.240
ecosystem with GCP.

00:22:30.900 --> 00:22:32.150
MICAH BAKER: Thank you, Caleb.

00:22:32.150 --> 00:22:33.660
That was an awesome demo.

00:22:33.660 --> 00:22:35.495
It's been fun watching
the evolution of Open

00:22:35.495 --> 00:22:37.530
Match over the past year.

00:22:37.530 --> 00:22:39.660
In Google Cloud, we
really believe strongly

00:22:39.660 --> 00:22:43.180
in being engine agnostic,
platform agnostic.

00:22:43.180 --> 00:22:46.980
So part of our
vision for gaming is

00:22:46.980 --> 00:22:49.470
to make sure that you can
get your services running

00:22:49.470 --> 00:22:51.900
and reach as many
players as possible.

00:22:51.900 --> 00:22:54.210
So you may have seen the
announcement for Stadia

00:22:54.210 --> 00:22:57.780
yesterday, which works really
well with Google Cloud.

00:22:57.780 --> 00:23:00.570
But we're not only
powering Stadia.

00:23:00.570 --> 00:23:02.520
We can power any games.

00:23:02.520 --> 00:23:04.560
We're really ready
to scale up whatever

00:23:04.560 --> 00:23:07.560
workloads are thrown at us.

00:23:07.560 --> 00:23:10.830
If it's Stadia, we're on
the same private network

00:23:10.830 --> 00:23:13.320
as those Stadia
instances, which is great,

00:23:13.320 --> 00:23:16.170
because you can have
predictably low latency, really

00:23:16.170 --> 00:23:18.990
great value with no internet
egress, and great data

00:23:18.990 --> 00:23:21.600
security because that data
never gets onto the internet.

00:23:21.600 --> 00:23:24.300
But having that infrastructure
hosted on Google Cloud

00:23:24.300 --> 00:23:26.490
also enables you to
power the other platforms

00:23:26.490 --> 00:23:28.710
that you're targeting
for your game.

00:23:28.710 --> 00:23:31.860
We also have solutions
around data and analytics,

00:23:31.860 --> 00:23:33.570
figuring out player
insights, like which

00:23:33.570 --> 00:23:35.778
players are going to pay or
not pay in the next seven

00:23:35.778 --> 00:23:37.362
days, which players
are going to leave

00:23:37.362 --> 00:23:38.790
your game in the
next seven days,

00:23:38.790 --> 00:23:41.640
lots of great
opportunities to figure out

00:23:41.640 --> 00:23:45.353
how to optimize your game
and make a better experience.

00:23:45.353 --> 00:23:47.020
We have a great
relationship with Unity,

00:23:47.020 --> 00:23:50.640
and we really appreciate
our efforts together.

00:23:50.640 --> 00:23:54.780
And our friends at Multiplay
have done some really amazing

00:23:54.780 --> 00:23:56.620
work over the past 20 years.

00:23:56.620 --> 00:23:59.980
We're excited to see
what they can do next.

00:23:59.980 --> 00:24:03.980
So thank you very
much for joining us.

00:24:03.980 --> 00:24:05.590
I'm waiting for my
slide to advance.

00:24:05.590 --> 00:24:06.120
Sorry.

00:24:06.120 --> 00:24:07.440
There we go.

00:24:07.440 --> 00:24:09.450
If you want to get
involved in our community,

00:24:09.450 --> 00:24:10.860
please jump into Open Match.

00:24:10.860 --> 00:24:12.880
It's an open source community.

00:24:12.880 --> 00:24:14.610
It's easy to get involved.

00:24:14.610 --> 00:24:17.430
Maybe you just want
to ask some questions.

00:24:17.430 --> 00:24:18.690
Join the Slack channel.

00:24:18.690 --> 00:24:21.670
There's an email list if you're
just kind of poking around.

00:24:21.670 --> 00:24:23.430
If you want to be a
contributor, you're

00:24:23.430 --> 00:24:26.445
more than welcome to join
in and grab some issues.

00:24:26.445 --> 00:24:28.320
We're excited to see
what the community wants

00:24:28.320 --> 00:24:29.660
to do with this project.

00:24:33.010 --> 00:24:37.680
And finally, a part of our
vision for Google in general

00:24:37.680 --> 00:24:41.340
is to create, connect,
and scale your games.

00:24:41.340 --> 00:24:43.423
And so we're bringing the
best of Google together.

00:24:43.423 --> 00:24:45.923
And this is something you're
going to see a lot more from us

00:24:45.923 --> 00:24:47.080
coming forward.

00:24:47.080 --> 00:24:48.710
Thank you.

